72
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Well, in this week's podcast we're going to talk about yardsticks. That's right,

73
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,000
And I apologize in advance if it gets a little preachy, but it's something I've been thinking about lately

74
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:41,000
And that will be in scoutmastership in seven minutes or less, which is rarely seven minutes and rarely less,

75
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
But you know we're kind of stuck with it,

76
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Oh my goodness. Well, enough of this foolishness, Let's get started, shall we?

77
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

78
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:31,000
Here's something Baden Powell wrote in January of 1931 in a recurring feature that was in the Scouter magazine

79
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
in the Scouts Association of Great Britain. He wrote this:

80
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Yes, scouting is a game, but I sometimes wonder whether, with all of our pamphlets, rules, conferences and training classes.

81
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:47,000
we may not appear to be making it too serious a game.

82
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Our phalanx of instructional aids appear terribly formidable from the outside,

83
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,000
And when you come to look at something formidable then you miss the whole spirit and the whole joy of it.

84
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Your boys catch the depression from you and scouting, having lost its spirit, is no longer a game for them.

85
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Scouting is not a science to be solemnly studied, nor is it a collection of doctrines and texts,

86
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:18,000
nor again is it a military code for drilling discipline into boys and repressing their individuality and initiative.

87
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:26,000
No, it is a jolly game in the out of doors where boy, men and boys can go adventuring together as older and younger brother,

88
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
picking up health, happiness, handicraft and helpfulness.

89
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:36,000
If you follow Baden Powell's blog that I publish every Sunday, or you take the time to read some of the things that he wrote,

90
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:45,000
you'll recognize what I just quoted Baden Powell as writing as being a pretty consistent theme in what Baden Powell had to say about scouting,

91
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And I think, if I was to paraphrase it, he was basically saying: you know, chill out, take it easy. all right,

92
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
This is not a science, it's a game, It's a lot of fun.

93
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Take a step back and get some perspective and help your scouts grow through enjoying playing this jolly game, as he said.

94
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Now I got to say, in the 10 or so years that I've been blogging and podcasting,

95
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:17,000
most of the questions and comments I receive concern very practical, pragmatic matters,

96
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:26,000
such as how we measure progress, or how we measure the indications that our programs are successful,

97
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:33,000
or how many inches this patch goes from that path, or when something should be worn or not be worn on the uniform

98
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and how many campaign hats dance on this particular pinhead.

99
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,000
You know what I'm saying. There's a lot of procedural things,

100
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:48,000
There's a lot of logistical, practical questions, and I'm happy to answer them,

101
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
But, as I do, I joined Baden Powell- and here I quote him again- in saying:

102
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:59,000
sometimes I wonder whether, with all our pamphlets, rules, conferences and training classes,

103
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
we may not appear to be making scouting a little too serious again.

104
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Now, the work that we're doing is, in one respect, very serious, because we are entrusted with working

105
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
with a very precious resource in the young people that we work with,

106
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:21,000
And our aim is to help them shape their character and to help them shape themselves

107
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
into really good, useful human beings,

108
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,000
And there are lots and lots of ways to get that right,

109
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
And there's lots of ways to get it wrong.

110
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
There's lots of ways of missing the big picture.

111
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,000
And as I was thinking about this, I got to thinking about systems of measurement.

112
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
The picture that came to my mind was a yardstick,

113
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
And what I want to say is: scouting really is not a yardstick,

114
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
It's not a system of measurement.

115
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:53,000
But wait a minute. you might say I just went to training and they handed me a whole bundle of yardstick.

116
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:58,000
Every time I look in a Scoutmaster handbook or I look in a Scout handbook or I look at anything,

117
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
it seems like there are an awful lot of yardsticks there.

118
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:07,000
This is the season where we're doing the journey to excellence, evaluations for our units-

119
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
That's a big old yardstick too.

120
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
And if you look at the advancement program, well, that's just yardsticks everywhere, right,

121
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Measurements and standards and rules, regulations, policies and procedures,

122
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
And these things are useful. okay,

123
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:25,000
I am not saying that they are in of themselves objectively bad.

124
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,000
It's just that often we get so distracted by them

125
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:35,000
that we forget about the spirit of this simple jolly game that Baden Powell started.

126
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
I mean, we have yardsticks for participation,

127
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:44,000
We have yardsticks for Scout spirit, which is really strange, isn't it?

128
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:51,000
I mean, we actually try to measure spirit, which is kind of an absurd contradiction in terms.

129
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:58,000
The risk of this being too obscure a reference: those of you who are fans of the movie Office Space: okay,

130
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:03,000
You'll remember that one of the characters in that movie worked in a restaurant

131
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and had to wear buttons and badges and things like that on their server's uniform

132
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
that were called Pieces of Flare.

133
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:17,000
And there was this big thing about: well, you know, you can have so many Pieces of Flare as a minimum,

134
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
but we'd really like to see you have more Pieces of Flare.

135
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:26,000
And it's actually a pretty funny sequence in the film because they're trying to get her

136
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,000
to have some spirit and vivacity and interest in her job with a number.

137
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And I think a lot of times we'll find ourselves trying to encourage or to excite Scouts

138
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:46,000
by using yardsticks, not to beat them with. but you know, measurements and things like that

139
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And, as I said, these things that were given the policies, procedures, rules, blah, blah, blah, blah the yardsticks.

140
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:57,000
they are not objectively bad. They're useful to us

141
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Because obviously in any game you have to have some system of measurement

142
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
to see whether or not you're advancing right.

143
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:07,000
I mean, in football. it's marked right on the field.

144
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
But allegories like the fact that scouting is a game

145
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
are not meant to carry the full weight of understanding every single thing about scouting,

146
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
because allegories break down eventually.

147
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,000
They can help describe certain aspects of things, but eventually, if you try and describe everything

148
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,000
about a particular pursuit using an allegory, you're going to break down at one point or another.

149
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:35,000
So let's leave the allegory aside for a moment and talk about how we work with those measurements in scouting.

150
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:40,000
I heard from somebody this week who was touting their effectiveness as a scouter

151
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
by saying that they really cracked down on their scouts

152
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:48,000
and they made them. they made sure that they learned all the skills

153
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and they did that by testing and retesting them during Boards of Review

154
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:57,000
and they basically had a system set up so all their scouts became first class by a certain age

155
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
and they pushed them to become Eagle Scouts by a certain age

156
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
and they had a fantastic spirited bunch

157
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
and, by gosh, they were making men out of boys.

158
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,000
It was a very aggressive style of scouting

159
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and I pointed out that the attitude and some of the way that they were very much inconsistent

160
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,000
with some clearly expressed policies

161
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
and they said, yeah, well, we know better.

162
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
We know better, we don't need those yardsticks, we don't need those measurements

163
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
because we know better how to measure these things.

164
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:37,000
So that's one extreme of a poor use of these systems of measurement. right,

165
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
By taking every single scout, matching them up against the yardstick,

166
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
by applying it ruthlessly,

167
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
We're going to have a standardized way of approaching this.

168
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
You're all going to get first class at this point in time,

169
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
you're all going to get Eagle this point in time

170
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
and by golly, you're going to know exactly how to do everything

171
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
in a way that we can objectively measure.

172
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
And, by the way, that particular individual was very proud of the fact

173
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
that they didn't hand out participation ribbons.

174
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
They had clear winners and losers, you know.

175
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:09,000
So, hey, I'm sure they're probably not doing objective harm to young people,

176
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:13,000
but they're missing the point of scouting. as far as I'm concerned.

177
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
And you know, it's very possible to swing to the other extreme

178
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
of not having any measurement system at all. okay,

179
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:26,000
And just kind of say: well, you know, this is a youth led operation

180
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
and whatever they decide to do is what we're going to do.

181
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
and they don't seem to be particularly interested

182
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
in playing the game according to the rules.

183
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
So you know, it's their decision. right,

184
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
We're just going to kind of go along and do that

185
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
and we'll go to the amusement park

186
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,000
and we'll have movie night and we'll play basketball,

187
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
and you know that's equally outside of the game of scouting.

188
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
that reaches the aims of scouting.

189
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
So where are we so far? okay,

190
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
Yes, we're presented with systems of measurement.

191
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,000
We're presented with yardsticks in the scouting program

192
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:04,000
and the ways of rules, regulations, requirements, procedures, methods

193
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
and other systems of measurement.

194
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,000
We can go to one extreme and consider that there are objective right measurements

195
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
for every single thing.

196
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
We can go to the other extreme and say,

197
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
well, you know, there's really no objective right measurement

198
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
for every single thing.

199
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
There's really no reason to use any of these systems of measurement

200
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:27,000
because our scouts are going to do whatever they want to do anyway

201
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
and we want to make sure they're entertained

202
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
and that they do entertaining things.

203
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
But, as you may imagine, between those two extremes

204
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
is a much more effective way of doing things

205
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
To step away from the allegories for a moment

206
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
and understand scouting for what it is.

207
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
because scouting happens one scout at a time.

208
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
It does not happen to a troop,

209
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
It does not happen to a patrol.

210
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
What scouting is meant to achieve

211
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,000
happens for one scout at a time.

212
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Since I began by reading something that Ben Powell wrote-

213
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:05,000
I'll mention this to. another big theme in his body of writings

214
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:10,000
is that scouting is for an individual scout.

215
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:14,000
It's meant to help them grow individually

216
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and to develop their character and sense of service

217
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
on an individual basis.

218
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
The most important yardstick is the one that we create

219
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
with our scouts on an individual basis,

220
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
And this is hard to get.

221
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
I started with the allegory of yardsticks

222
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
and now it's going to fall apart,

223
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
because now what I'm suggesting is

224
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
that from an objective thing

225
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
like an inch is an inch, of foot is a foot

226
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
and a yard is a yard,

227
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
is that there's a very subjective standard

228
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
and let's call it progress,

229
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:51,000
And that subjective standard

230
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,000
is measured one scout at a time.

231
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:57,000
because what we ask ourselves is: where did they begin?

232
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Where did they begin on this continuum of development

233
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
we call scouting, and where are they now?

234
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Have they advanced, Have they improved,

235
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
Have they moved forward? Then we're successful

236
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
and we're not going to be able to measure

237
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,000
the distance in inches and feet and yards and miles.

238
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
we're only going to be able to measure that

239
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
by looking at them individually,

240
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
having a conversation with them

241
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
and seeing what they understand.

242
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
and on that one thing:

243
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
on this concept of individual progress,

244
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
not just in advancement,

245
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
not just in participation,

246
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
not just in spirit, but in character.

247
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
the development of character

248
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
is not something you're going to be able to assess

249
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
with an objective, external measurement.

250
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:46,000
it's going to be one that's a lot more tricky to assess

251
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:49,000
because you're going to have to sit and talk to a scout about it.

252
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
and for me personally,

253
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,000
this idea has been the key that's unlocked

254
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
many, many doors and shed light

255
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
on tremendous opportunities

256
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
as a scouter, to help my scouts

257
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
better understand themselves

258
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,000
and the way that they relate to the world around them

259
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and to encourage them to grow

260
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
in character and a sense of service

261
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and usefulness to others,

262
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:15,000
because they're not competing against every other scout,

263
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
to develop the best character

264
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
they're competing against where they began

265
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
and where they finally will end up.

266
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and to figure out that measurement.

267
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
like I said, it takes sitting down

268
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
and talking to a scout

269
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
and working along with them

270
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
and finding out what they understand.

271
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
how do you understand the scout, oath and law?

272
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
tell me a little bit about that,

273
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
and then we're going to do that again

274
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
in months or years to come

275
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
and we're going to ask the same question

276
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
and we're going to note that,

277
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
yeah, the activities of

278
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
playing this wonderful jolly game of scouting

279
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
has given them more understanding

280
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
about these things,

281
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
has helped them build their character

282
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
and their sense of usefulness

283
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
and their self-esteem, all at the same time.

284
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
so yardsticks

285
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and systems of measurement in scouting

286
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
are there for a good reason.

287
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
they help us define the program,

288
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
but they can't really objectively

289
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
define the progress

290
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
that an individual makes in scouting.

291
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
to do that, we can't apply a yardstick.

292
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
we can't measure that in feet

293
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
and inches, in yards.

294
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
we have to talk to our scouts

295
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
and bring them to a shared understanding

296
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
of the things that they're developing

297
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
through their participation in scouting:

298
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:35,000
music,

299
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:38,000
music.

